{"name":"Cottage White","hex":"#f7efdd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cottage","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small house.","A seasonal home of any size or stature, a recreational home or a home in a remote location."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To stay at a seasonal home, to go cottaging.","To have homosexual sex in a public lavatory; to practice cottaging."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cottage"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cottage","blocks":[{"text":"A cottage during England's feudal period was the holding by a cottager of a small house with enough garden to feed a family and in return for the cottage, the cottager had to provide some form of service to the manorial lord. However, in time cottage just became the general term for a small house. In modern usage, a cottage is usually a modest, often cosy dwelling, typically in a rural or semi-rural location and not necessarily in England. The cottage orné, often quite large and grand residences built by the nobility, dates back to a movement of \"rustic\" stylised cottages of the late 18th and early 19th century during the Romantic movement."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Country_cottage%2C_Inch_Island_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3951065_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Country_cottage%2C_Inch_Island_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3951065_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cottage","wikidata":"Q5783996"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/South_Devon_cottage_-_geograph.org.uk_-_81799.jpg/500px-South_Devon_cottage_-_geograph.org.uk_-_81799.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/South_Devon_cottage_-_geograph.org.uk_-_81799.jpg/500px-South_Devon_cottage_-_geograph.org.uk_-_81799.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A typical cottage in Devon, with walls built of cob and a thatched roof","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASouth_Devon_cottage_-_geograph.org.uk_-_81799.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Miners_Cottages%2C_Pit_Village%2C_Beamish_Museum%2C_5_September_2013.jpg/500px-Miners_Cottages%2C_Pit_Village%2C_Beamish_Museum%2C_5_September_2013.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Miners_Cottages%2C_Pit_Village%2C_Beamish_Museum%2C_5_September_2013.jpg/500px-Miners_Cottages%2C_Pit_Village%2C_Beamish_Museum%2C_5_September_2013.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnai